843	/// Edit ▸ Cut / Copy / Paste / Paste Attributes (⌘X, ⌘C, ⌘V, ⌥⌘V) — the
844	/// four menu items `EditorViewController.swift` forwards here — and the
845	/// fifth, Edit ▸ Paste Graphic Properties, whose action is this file's own.
846	///
847	/// The clipboard is one pasteboard type and two ops that read it. Copy writes
848	/// the selection as the `paste` op's payload (the module doc of
849	/// `core/src/edit_paste.rs` is its schema) under `com.kgalligan.framejob.clips`;
850	/// Paste hands that payload straight back to the core, which does all the
851	/// deciding — fresh ids, media matched by path, the overlap rule, the frame
852	/// grid — and Paste Attributes and Paste Graphic Properties hand the SAME
853	/// payload to `paste_attributes`, which reads only its earliest clip's
854	/// property groups. Nothing about a clip is interpreted on this side.
855	///
856	/// **Every reader and writer takes its pasteboard as a parameter**, the
857	/// general one by default, so a probe can drive Copy and both pastes over a
858	/// private pasteboard and never touch the owner's clipboard.
859	///
860	/// Two decisions worth stating, because neither is forced:
861	///
862	/// **The payload is built from the core's own project JSON, not from
863	/// `ProjectSnapshot`.** The snapshot is a LENIENT read view (its `init(from:)`
864	/// falls back rather than failing), so re-encoding one would quietly drop
865	/// whatever this build does not know about and round every field through
866	/// Swift's idea of the schema. Copying the raw JSON objects means a pasted
867	/// clip is the clip that was copied, down to the keys nobody has written yet.
868	///
869	/// **A payload clip's `track` is rewritten to a DEPTH from the front of the
870	/// stack** (0 = front-most), because a track id means nothing in another
871	/// document — and pasting between documents is the case the private
872	/// pasteboard type exists for. Depth is the only thing about the source's
873	/// track stack worth carrying: it is what makes a two-lane selection paste
874	/// back onto two lanes.
875	extension EditorViewController {
876	    /// The private pasteboard type the payload travels under. Private on
877	    /// purpose: a Frame Job clip is not a flavour of anything else, and a paste
878	    /// from another app is a beep, not a guess.
879	    static let clipsPasteboardType = NSPasteboard.PasteboardType("com.kgalligan.framejob.clips")
880	
881	    // MARK: - Copy
882	
883	    /// ⌘C. Writes the selection to the pasteboard (the general one, unless a
884	    /// probe names its own), with a short human summary as the plain-text
885	    /// fallback — pasting into a text editor should say what was copied, not
886	    /// spill a project.
887	    ///
888	    /// Returns whether anything was written, so Cut can stop before it deletes
889	    /// something it failed to copy.
890	    @discardableResult
891	    func copySelection(to pasteboard: NSPasteboard = .general) -> Bool {
892	        guard let document = document, !document.selection.isEmpty,
893	              let payload = clipboardPayload(for: document),
894	              JSONSerialization.isValidJSONObject(payload),
895	              let data = try? JSONSerialization.data(
896	                withJSONObject: payload, options: [.sortedKeys])
897	        else {
898	            NSSound.beep()
899	            return false
900	        }
901	        // `declareTypes` clears the pasteboard and takes ownership in one
902	        // call, which is the documented pairing for `setData(_:forType:)`.
903	        pasteboard.declareTypes([Self.clipsPasteboardType, .string], owner: nil)
904	        pasteboard.setData(data, forType: Self.clipsPasteboardType)
905	        pasteboard.setString(clipboardSummary(for: document), forType: .string)
906	        return true
907	    }
